.\" Keep this page current: it describes the demo game demos/river2.bas (an executable made with BUILD, installed by "make install").
.TH ENGINE1999-RIVER2 6 "November 2026" "Engine 1999 @VERSION@" "Engine1999 Manual"
.SH NAME
engine1999-river2 \- Eternal River II, a scroll-down boat game
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B engine1999-river2
.SH DESCRIPTION
Eternal River II is the second edition. New: a hot air balloon of 3 x 3 sprites (a sprite group) with a translucent shadow drifts across the river on a sprite path, and flocks of gulls cross it; they cry and roar through events that their paths report (ON SPRITE). Eternal River is a scroll-down boat game in the spirit of River Raid. You steer a boat down a river that meanders and never ends. Swimmers, divers and other boats are in the way, and people on the banks throw rocks at you. Now and then a bridge crosses the river, with people, a bicycle and cars on it, just for decoration: you pass under it (and score 100 points). The banks are lined with meadows, trees, villages and roads, and wraiths walk about on them, some on horses: black and white and see-through. If you are on land and touch one, you become a wraith too (with or without a horse) and lose a life. As a wraith you can still collect goodies and nothing can harm you, but after a while you vanish and, if you have lives left, start again in a new boat.
.PP
A hit is a matter of luck: you may be wrecked, or you may only be thrown into the water. Then swim to a bank (the swimming time is limited), walk along it up and down (the river scrolls as you walk, back as far as two screens; trees are no obstacle, houses are), pick up the apples, carrots and ice cream cones you find, and look for a boat: one moored at a jetty, or one that was left drifting. The river gets narrower and busier with the distance (every 4000 pixels is a new level), and you get an extra life for every 5000 points.
.PP
The program is the demo \fIriver2.bas\fR of Engine1999, made into an executable with
.B BUILD
(window kind: it needs the SDL2 library, nothing else); its BASIC libraries are inside it. It opens the Engine1999 window; the window can
be switched between the full screen and a window with F11 or ALT+RETURN.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP
.BR LEFT / RIGHT " or " A / D
steer the boat (swim, walk).
.TP
.BR UP " or " W
faster; walking: walk up (the river scrolls).
.TP
.BR DOWN " or " S
slower; walking: walk down (the river scrolls back).
.TP
.B SPACE
walking: jump into the water.
.TP
.B P
pause.
.TP
.B Q
quit.
.PP
A game controller works, too: D-pad or left stick steers, A jumps, START pauses, BACK quits.
.SH FILES
The best score is kept in the file
.I river2.hi
in the current directory (the game goes on without it if the file cannot be written). The current directory is outside the folder of the game, so Engine1999 asks once for permission to write there; answer "Always" to keep the high score (see
.BR engine1999\-basic (7),
SECURITY).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR engine1999 (1),
.BR engine1999-basic (7),
.BR engine1999-river (6)
